# Tablet: QR code on the virtual desktop

When the user is on a **tablet** (Cursor in the browser) and wants the current app on their **phone**, follow this exactly.

The tablet keyboard often does not open in the terminal. Do not ask them to type a command. Do not paste a QR code into the chat as text, ANSI blocks, or base64.

## What they should see

A large QR code on the **virtual machine desktop** (the desktop on the right). They scan it with the phone camera. It opens the **live** app in the phone browser and updates as the code changes. This is not the GitHub Pages preview, which only updates after a merge.

## How

1. Serve the app with Metro on port **8081** (`npm run start:dev-client` or `npx expo start --web`). Web is what the phone opens. There is no Cursor Android app, and a Play or preview install does not load this live session.
2. The phone cannot reach the VM’s private address. Publish port 8081 with a public HTTPS tunnel. A Cloudflare quick tunnel works without an Expo login:

   ```bash
   cloudflared tunnel --url http://127.0.0.1:8081 --no-autoupdate
   ```

3. Build a real QR image for that public `https://…` URL. Do not invent the image.
4. On the virtual desktop (`DISPLAY=:1`), open a large white window in front of everything. Title it so it is obvious. Show “Scan with your phone”, the QR at a size that fills much of the screen, and the URL underneath.
5. Leave that window up. If the tunnel URL changes, replace the QR. Tell them the code is on the desktop.

One way that already works: serve a small HTML page on localhost and open it with Chrome on `DISPLAY=:1`:

```bash
google-chrome --app=http://127.0.0.1:<port>/pelvipilot-qr.html --window-size=1920,1200 --window-position=0,0 --start-maximized
```

After a code change, keep the same tunnel URL when you can so the phone stays on the live app. Ask them to check **Settings** at the bottom for the version name when a change should show up there.
